# Real-time Exam Proctoring with YOLO11-Pose

A computer vision system that monitors multiple camera streams during exams and flags
suspicious student behaviour (head turns, looking away, leaving the frame) using
human pose estimation.

> University project · Computer Vision course

---

## Overview

Instead of training a custom classifier on raw images, the system relies on **pose keypoints**
(nose, eyes, ears, shoulders) produced by **YOLO11-pose**. Simple, interpretable geometric
metrics are computed from these keypoints (head yaw/offset relative to shoulders, keypoint
visibility). A **temporal warning accumulator** then decides whether the behaviour is really
suspicious or just a short random movement.

The pipeline is built as a distributed system: camera producers push frames to **Redis**,
and inference workers consume them independently. This makes it easy to scale to many
cameras.

## Key features

- **Pose-based behaviour analysis.** YOLO11-pose keypoints are turned into head-orientation metrics.
- **Temporal warning logic.** Warnings are raised only when suspicious behaviour persists over
  several frames, which reduces false positives from single noisy detections.
- **Multi-camera pipeline.** Producers push frames to Redis and workers run inference in parallel.
- **Camera emulation.** Any set of video files can be replayed as live camera streams for testing.
- **Interfaces.** A Streamlit web dashboard, plus a desktop viewer with a separate warnings window.
- **Model comparison.** YOLO11 (l / x) pose models compared against a Faster R-CNN baseline.
- **Dockerized.** Redis and the workers start with `docker-compose`.
- **Tested.** A pytest suite covers pose metrics, warning accumulation and frame decoding.

## Architecture
```
┌──────────────┐     ┌──────────┐     ┌───────────┐     ┌───────────────────┐
│ Cameras /    │     │ producer │     │   Redis   │     │ worker            │
│ video files  ├────►│ (encode) ├────►│  (queue)  ├────►│ YOLO11-pose       │
└──────────────┘     └──────────┘     └───────────┘     │ + pose metrics    │
                                                        │ + warning logic   │
                                                        └─────────┬─────────┘
                                                                  │
                                    ┌─────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┐
                                    ▼                             ▼                  ▼
                           Streamlit dashboard           Desktop viewer      Post-processing
                                                        + warnings window   (evidence clips)
```

## Results

The models were evaluated on recorded classroom videos with manually annotated suspicious episodes.

| Model                    | Accuracy (approx.) | Notes                                    |
|--------------------------|--------------------|------------------------------------------|
| YOLO11l-pose             | ~70–80%            | Nearly the same quality as the x model   |
| YOLO11x-pose             | ~70–80%            | Larger model gave no significant gain    |
| Faster R-CNN (baseline)  | lower              | Detection only, no keypoints             |

### Key finding

**Model size was not the bottleneck.** Moving from YOLO11l to YOLO11x barely changed the
accuracy. Most errors came from the **input data**, not from the model:

1. **Camera angle.** With high or side-mounted cameras, faces and shoulders are seen at steep
   angles. Head-turn metrics become ambiguous, and some keypoints are occluded or not detected.
2. **Video quality.** Low resolution, compression artifacts and poor lighting produce
   low-confidence, "jittery" keypoints. Random shifts of the nose keypoint look like head turns
   and cause false positives.

This means that better camera placement (frontal, at eye level) and higher-quality streams
would likely improve the results more than a heavier model.

## Tech stack

| Area           | Tools                                       |
|----------------|---------------------------------------------|
| CV / ML        | Ultralytics YOLO11-pose, Faster R-CNN, OpenCV |
| Inference      | PyTorch, ONNX, TensorRT                     |
