| UI             | Streamlit, desktop viewer                   |
| Testing        | pytest                                      |

## Quick start
```bash
# 1. Install dependencies
pip install -r requirements.txt

# 2. Start Redis
docker-compose up -d redis

# 3. Emulate camera streams from demo videos
python tools/camerasEmulation.py

# 4. Run an inference worker
python src/worker.py

# 5. Open the dashboard
streamlit run apps/streamlit_app.py
```

Model weights (`yolo11l-pose.pt`, `yolo11x-pose.pt`) are downloaded automatically by Ultralytics
on first run. To use them offline, place them in `models/`.

### Run tests
```bash
pytest tests/
```

## Project structure
```
src/           core pipeline: proctoring engine, workers, viewers, training script
apps/          Streamlit dashboard and web interface
tools/         camera emulation, frame producer, post-processing, visualisation
experiments/   Faster R-CNN baseline for comparison
tests/         pytest suite (pose metrics, warning logic, frame decoding)
docs/          method description and known issues
assets/demo/   short demo video
models/        model weights (not tracked in git)
```

## Limitations

- Accuracy depends heavily on camera placement. Steep angles break the head-orientation metrics.
- Low-confidence keypoints on low-quality video produce false positives.
- Rule-based metrics need manual threshold tuning for each classroom setup.

## Future work

- **Keypoint smoothing.** Apply a Kalman or One-Euro filter and ignore low-confidence keypoints
  to cut jitter-based false positives.
- **Temporal model.** Train a small LSTM or 1D-CNN on keypoint sequences instead of hand-crafted thresholds.
- **Per-camera calibration.** Normalise metrics for the camera angle.
- **Gaze estimation.** Add a dedicated head-pose or gaze model for frontal cameras.
- **Proper benchmark.** Build a larger annotated dataset and report precision, recall and F1
  separately for each camera setup.

